md=['# Bangladesh library expansion: ten verified academic targets','', '**Result:** ten recommended institutions and one reserve returned real book records and item holdings through public catalog searches on **15 November2026 UTC**. All11 use Koha. These are a feasible integration shortlist, **not a defensible claim to be the country’s ten largest libraries**: several substantially larger institutions have inaccessible or robots-restricted catalogs. No app provider was configured, enabled or deployed.','', '## Marketplace and what unlocks the most','', 'Koha offers the strongest demonstrated adapter reuse. Every successful catalog here uses its standard public CGI search/detail pattern. The existing MyBookshelf generic Koha parser passed offline replay for the ten targets; CUET and RUET failed the richer title parser and need parser work for that route. This observed, feasibility-selected sample does not establish national LMS market share. The evidence supports “best first platform,” not a fabricated national percentage.','', 'The public-library opportunity is distinct from the ready academic catalogs. A [March2026 study](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44217-026-01315-1) surveyed59 professionals from48 of71 government public libraries and found substantial reliance on manual workflows and infrastructure gaps. Its percentages should not be treated as a deployment census: respondents and institutions are different denominators. The government also already offers [Amar Library](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.misfit.publiclibrary), updated August2025, claiming centralized government catalog search, physical-book availability, membership and interlibrary transfers. Its official site, http://elibrary.dpl.gov.bd/, timed out from this environment; no API or actual book query was verified. This is the strongest potential public-reader network partnership to investigate, while independent Koha catalogs are the proven near-term route. Existing initiatives mean the opportunity is complementary discovery/interoperability, not an assumption that no national digital service exists.','', 'The live [BANSDOC National Union Catalog](https://nucb.bansdoc.gov.bd/) displayed113 participating libraries,5,373 journals and9,563 holdings. It is a **journal** union catalog. Those113 participants must not be counted as immediately unlockable book libraries. UGC Digital Library/LiCoB subscription e-resource arrangements likewise do not prove public physical-book federation. CUET’s shared catalog exposes central/hall/seminar libraries; City exposes two campuses and Jagannath Central/CSE. Count these as branches within institutions, not extra independent targets.','', '## Ten verified targets','', 'The shortlist is ordered approximately by reported book count; the figures have different dates, units and evidence quality and do not establish national ranks. Nine targets have official count evidence; Jagannath uses a dated secondary newspaper report. Sources were accessed 15 November 2026: RUET has a 2 August 2025 university statement, Jagannath an October 2025 Prothom Alo report, and other pages are undated. IUB and CUET use indexed official text because direct retrieval did not expose the count; they measure different things (volumes,copies,books), and are not summed into a market-size claim. The shortlist is academic: institutional eligibility must be shown before promising borrowing.','', '| Library; city | Scale and source | Actual title query; returned books | Holdings observed | Catalog result |','|---|---|---|---|---|']
